{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Educational Technology Evaluation", "title": "Usability, Accessibility, and Equity", "description": "CAT 531 textbook section connecting usability, accessibility, and equity to equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "course": "cat531-supplement", "chapter": "05", "section": "03", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."], "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation", "cat531_supplement_usability_accessibility_and_equity", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "prereq_section_ids": ["cat531-supplement/05/02"], "practice_ids": ["cat531_supplement_5_3_usability_accessibility_and_equity_artifact_signal", "cat531_supplement_5_3_usability_accessibility_and_equity_annotation_signal", "cat531_supplement_5_3_usability_accessibility_and_equity_ai_boundary", "cat531_supplement_5_3_usability_accessibility_and_equity_calibration"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 3, "top_tier_hardened": false}, "content": "# Usability, Accessibility, and Equity\n\n![Usability, Accessibility, and Equity textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/05-03-usability-accessibility-and-equity.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\n\"Easy to use\" is the praise teachers reach for first, and it is the one that hides the most. A tool can be effortless for the typical student in the front row and impassable for the student using a screen reader, and a separate set of students can find it perfectly usable and perfectly accessible yet still be locked out by a home-internet requirement. Usability, accessibility, and equity are three different questions, and collapsing them into one word is how access problems pass an evaluation unnoticed. This section keeps them apart on purpose.\n\nThe stakes are not abstract. When the three checks collapse into \"looks fine,\" the students who fail the unasked checks are usually the students the system already underserves: disabled students, multilingual newcomers, students without reliable devices or connectivity. Pulling the three apart is what makes an evaluation an equity instrument rather than a comfort report.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Define usability, accessibility, and equity as three distinct checks with three distinct populations.\n- Run each check as its own test rather than inferring all three from a single smooth experience.\n- Recognize when a compliance badge (for example WCAG AA) does not match the tool's core task.\n- Document a decision that names which check failed, for whom, and what condition must be met before adoption.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA sixth-grade team evaluates a science-simulation app for a class that includes 2 students who use screen readers and several students without reliable home internet. A teacher runs the app, finds it quick and intuitive, and writes \"very easy, students click through fast, adopt.\" The note is true and almost entirely beside the point for the students who matter most here.\n\nThe decision worth recording is not \"the app is easy.\" It is whether the team checked the same tasks with a screen reader (accessibility) and against the connectivity and home-practice demands (equity), and what they found when they did.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThe three checks answer three separate questions about three populations:\n\n- **Usability** asks whether a typical user can accomplish the task efficiently and without confusion. Its population is the median student.\n- **Accessibility** asks whether students using assistive technology or with disabilities can accomplish the same core task. Its population is disabled students, and the relevant standard is the core activity, not the menus.\n- **Equity** asks whether the tool imposes burdens unrelated to ability that fall unevenly across students, such as device ownership, home connectivity, cost, or language. Its population is whoever the tool's hidden requirements exclude.\n\n| Check | Question | Population | Common failure |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Usability | Can a typical user do the task easily? | Median student | Confusing flow |\n| Accessibility | Can an assistive-tech user do the core task? | Disabled students | Unlabeled controls on the main activity |\n| Equity | Does the tool burden some students unfairly? | The excluded | Home-internet or device dependency |\n\nTwo traps recur. The first is the **badge mismatch**: a tool advertises WCAG AA, but the badge covers its navigation while the core learning activity (a drag-and-drop lab) has no spoken labels. Compliance on the shell is not compliance on the task. The second is **collapsing equity into accessibility**: a home-internet requirement is not an accessibility flaw, it is an equity flaw, and treating them as one hides it.\n\nThree course anchors guide the checks. **TPACK** keeps the accessibility test focused on the core learning task rather than the login screen. **Critical edtech studies** names the equity burdens vendors externalize onto families (devices, data plans, nightly practice). **AI and education datafication** flags tools whose \"adaptivity\" degrades for students with less data history, an equity failure dressed as personalization.\n\nBefore running the checks on a real tool, fix the three apart in your mind. Match each check to the population it is actually about and the direct test that exposes its failure. The point is to stop \"easy to use\" from absorbing the other two.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Usability\",\"definition\":\"Median student; test: can a typical sighted mouse user finish the task without confusion?\"},{\"term\":\"Accessibility\",\"definition\":\"Disabled students; test: run the core lab with a screen reader and check for spoken labels on the activity, not the menus\"},{\"term\":\"Equity\",\"definition\":\"Whoever the hidden requirements exclude; test: list what the tool demands off-screen (home internet, a device, money, English) that some students cannot supply\"},{\"term\":\"Badge mismatch trap\",\"definition\":\"A WCAG AA label that covers navigation while the drag-and-drop lab has no spoken labels\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A teacher runs a science sim as a sighted mouse user, finds it fast and intuitive, and writes 'very easy, adopt.' Which of the three checks has this trial actually evidenced?\" explanation=\"Only usability, and only for the median student. Ease for a sighted mouse user says nothing about whether a screen-reader user can complete the core lab (accessibility) or whether a student without home internet loses work (equity). A single smooth trial cannot stand in for all three checks.\" options='[{\"text\":\"All three; if it is easy it is also accessible and equitable.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Only usability, for the median student; accessibility and equity are still untested.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Accessibility, because the interface was clear.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nThe weak version below sounds plausible, but it does not yet give a reader enough evidence:\n\n> I made the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes so that the intended audience can see the decision, the constraint, and the evidence source. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies**, and I can point to the exact part of the artifact that changed.\n\nThe improvement is not longer wording. It is a better trace: the reader can see the artifact, the constraint, and the exact revision that followed.\n\n### Worked Example: Usable, Accessible, and Equitable Are Three Checks\n\nScenario: a sixth-grade team evaluates a science-simulation app for a class that includes 2 students who use screen readers and several students without reliable home internet.\n\n1. **Usability check (before).** A teacher tries the app and notes \"very easy, students click through fast.\" The early annotation reads \"looks fine, adopt.\"\n2. **Accessibility check.** Running the same tasks with a screen reader, the team finds the drag-and-drop lab steps give no spoken labels, so the 2 screen-reader users cannot complete the core activity. The vendor's \"WCAG AA\" badge did not match the task.\n3. **Equity check.** The app also auto-saves only when online and assigns nightly home practice. Students without home internet would lose work, a burden unrelated to accessibility.\n4. **Annotated judgment call (after).** The revised entry reads: \"Usable for typical users: yes. Accessible for screen-reader users on the core lab: no (drag steps unlabeled). Equity risk: home-internet dependency for nightly saves. Decision: request the vendor's keyboard-accessible lab mode and move practice to in-class time before adopting. We rejected 'easy to use' as sufficient because it answered only the first of three checks.\"\n\nThe brief is defensible because each of the three checks is named separately and the trade-off is explicit rather than collapsed into one word.\n\nNow make the call yourself. The scenario hands you the same sixth-grade science-sim decision, with 2 screen-reader users and several students without home internet. Each choice tests whether you keep the three checks apart or let one absorb the others.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The smooth science sim, the screen reader, and the home-internet gap\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"You run the science-simulation app and it is fast and intuitive. Your class has 2 students who use screen readers and several without reliable home internet. What do you write first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"\\\"Very easy, students click through fast, adopt\\\"\",\"next\":\"easy\",\"feedback\":\"You logged usability and treated it as the whole verdict.\"},{\"label\":\"Log usability narrowly, then run the core lab with a screen reader\",\"next\":\"screen\",\"feedback\":\"You refuse to let one smooth trial answer all three checks.\"}]},\"easy\":{\"prompt\":\"You adopt. Your 2 screen-reader users cannot complete the drag-and-drop lab; nothing is announced. What did your very-easy note actually measure?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Usability for the median student only; it never tested accessibility or equity\",\"next\":\"easy_end\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Ease for a sighted mouse user said nothing about the students most at risk.\"}]},\"easy_end\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: the phrase easy to use answered only the first of three checks. The screen-reader failure on the core lab and the home-internet equity risk passed through unexamined.\",\"feedback\":\"This is how access problems pass an evaluation: one word absorbs three questions.\",\"choices\":[]},\"screen\":{\"prompt\":\"With the screen reader, the drag steps give no spoken labels, so your 2 users cannot do the core activity. The vendor lists a WCAG AA badge. Does the badge rescue it?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes, WCAG AA means it is accessible\",\"next\":\"badge\",\"feedback\":\"You trusted the badge without checking what it covers.\"},{\"label\":\"No; the badge covers navigation, but the core lab itself is unlabeled\",\"next\":\"equity\",\"feedback\":\"You record the failure at the level of the activity, not the menu.\"}]},\"badge\":{\"prompt\":\"You re-test and find the badge applies only to the menus and login, not the drag-and-drop lab your students must use. Where does the accessibility failure actually live?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"On the core learning task, which the badge never covered\",\"next\":\"equity\",\"feedback\":\"Now the accessibility finding sits where it belongs.\"}]},\"equity\":{\"prompt\":\"You also notice the app auto-saves only when online and assigns nightly home practice. Students without home internet lose work. Where do you file this?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Under accessibility, with the screen-reader issue\",\"next\":\"equity_bad\",\"feedback\":\"You folded an equity burden into accessibility, where it disappears.\"},{\"label\":\"On its own equity line, as a burden unrelated to ability\",\"next\":\"resolve\",\"feedback\":\"You keep equity distinct from accessibility.\"}]},\"equity_bad\":{\"prompt\":\"Collapsing home-internet dependency into accessibility hides it; it is not a disability barrier. Re-file it.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Move it to a separate equity line\",\"next\":\"resolve\",\"feedback\":\"Now all three checks are visible and distinct.\"}]},\"resolve\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: usable for typical users yes; accessible on the core lab no (drag steps unlabeled); equity risk from home-internet saves. Decision: request a keyboard-accessible lab mode and move practice to class time before adopting.\",\"feedback\":\"Each failure becomes a named condition, not a shrug, and easy to use is rejected as sufficient.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nRun usability, accessibility, and equity as three checks in that order, and never let a pass on one stand in for the others. A tool is adoptable only when all three pass for the actual students in the room, or when a named condition makes them pass.\n\nWhen the tool feels smooth, run the three checks deliberately:\n\n1. **Usability check:** Can the median student complete the task without confusion?\n2. **Accessibility check:** Can your assistive-technology users complete the same core task, tested directly, not inferred from a badge?\n3. **Equity check:** What does the tool require (a device, home internet, money, English) that some of your students cannot supply?\n\nA failure on any check does not end the evaluation; it converts into a named condition (\"request keyboard-accessible lab mode,\" \"move practice to class time\") that must be met before adoption.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is reading a single smooth trial as evidence for all three checks; ease for the median student says nothing about the screen-reader user or the student without internet. The second is trusting a compliance badge without confirming it covers the core task rather than the shell. The third is filing an equity burden, such as required home connectivity, under accessibility, where it quietly disappears.\n\nA defensible matrix records the three checks on three lines, names the population each one is about, and converts each failure into a condition rather than a shrug.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTake the science app through all three lanes. Run the lab once as a sighted mouse user: smooth, so usability passes for the median student, and you log that narrowly. Now run the identical lab with a screen reader; the drag steps announce nothing, so accessibility fails on the core task, and the WCAG AA badge, which covered navigation, does not rescue it. You record the failure at the level of the activity, not the menu.\n\nThen leave the interface and look at what the tool assumes off-screen. It auto-saves only online and assigns nightly home practice, so a student without home internet loses work through no fault of ability. That is an equity failure, filed on its own line, not folded into the accessibility note.\n\nClose by turning both failures into conditions. The brief does not say \"reject\" or \"adopt\"; it says \"adopt only if the vendor enables a keyboard-accessible lab mode and we move practice to in-class time.\" A reviewer can now see exactly which students were at risk, on which check, and what would clear the path.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a three-row note rather than two columns: one row each for usability, accessibility, and equity, and in each row name the population and the specific test you would run. The exercise forces the checks apart and stops \"easy to use\" from absorbing the other two.\n\nA strong reader of this section can answer four questions:\n\n1. For each check, which population is it actually about?\n2. What direct test (not a badge, not a vibe) would I run for accessibility on the core task?\n3. What off-screen requirement might exclude some students even when the interface is fine?\n4. What condition would convert each failure into a defensible adoption?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nFor this section, the matrix is strong when usability, accessibility, and equity each occupy their own row, each names its population, and each failure carries a condition, so a mentor can see no check was inferred from another. Treat a clean usability impression as the weakest evidence in the matrix, because it speaks only for the students least at risk.\n\nA useful closing line would sound like this: \"I changed the matrix so accessibility and equity are scored separately from usability, each with its population named. I kept out the suggestion to record one overall ease score because it would have hidden the screen-reader failure on the core lab and the home-internet equity risk.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same app to a one-to-one high school with school-issued devices, campus wifi, and no screen-reader users this term. The equity burden (home internet) and the accessibility failure (no screen-reader user affected today) recede, and usability may now carry more of the decision. But the checks themselves do not retire: a transfer student who relies on assistive tech could enroll next week, and the accessibility defect is still real even when no current student trips on it. Use the shift to confirm that the three checks are properties of the tool and the room, recomputed for each setting, not a verdict you carry forward.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/05-03-usability-accessibility-and-equity-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"05.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **TPACK**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes for CAT 531 05.03. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation\",\"cat531_supplement_usability_accessibility_and_equity\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_usability_accessibility_and_equity\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm the matrix scores usability, accessibility, and equity on separate rows, names the population each check is about, tests accessibility on the core task rather than a badge, and turns every failure into a named condition for adoption.\n\n## References\n\n- TPACK. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- AI and education datafication. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_5_3_usability_accessibility_and_equity_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_usability_accessibility_and_equity", "stem": "A teacher reports: 'The app is very easy to use; even my youngest students click through it fast.' Why does this not yet establish accessibility?", "options": ["Ease of use is the same thing as accessibility.", "Usability for typical users does not show that students using screen readers, captions, or keyboard-only navigation can complete the same tasks.", "Accessibility only matters for physical buildings, not software.", "Fast clicking proves the tool works for everyone."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Usability describes the experience of typical users. Accessibility asks whether learners who rely on assistive technology or accommodations can do the same work. The first does not demonstrate the second.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_5_3_usability_equals_accessibility", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation", "cat531_supplement_usability_accessibility_and_equity", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_5_3_usability_accessibility_and_equity_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_usability_accessibility_and_equity", "stem": "A vendor says the tool 'meets WCAG AA.' What evidence would let a teacher trust that claim for their classroom?", "options": ["The vendor's marketing badge alone.", "An accessibility conformance report (such as a VPAT) plus a check of the specific tasks students will actually perform.", "The fact that the tool has many users.", "A confirmation that the homepage looks accessible."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A conformance label is a starting point, not proof. A documented report and a task-level check on the workflows students use are what make the claim usable for a decision.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_5_3_wcag_badge_is_proof", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation", "cat531_supplement_usability_accessibility_and_equity"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_5_3_usability_accessibility_and_equity_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "You ask an AI tool to run an accessibility audit on a learning app. It reports 'no accessibility issues found.' What is the defensible interpretation?", "options": ["The tool is fully accessible for all students.", "Automated checks catch only some barriers; you still need a task walkthrough with assistive technology and, where possible, disabled-user testing.", "Manual testing is now unnecessary.", "The AI result overrides any conformance report."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Automated audits detect a minority of accessibility barriers. Many barriers only surface in real use with a screen reader, keyboard, or actual disabled testers. The AI result is one input, not a clearance.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_5_3_automated_audit_is_complete", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning", "cat531_supplement_usability_accessibility_and_equity"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_5_3_usability_accessibility_and_equity_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "stem": "An app is accessible and easy to use, but it requires a reliable home internet connection for nightly homework. In an equity analysis, how should a teacher treat this?", "options": ["Ignore it, since the app itself is accessible.", "Flag it as an equity risk: a tool that is accessible can still disadvantage students without home connectivity, so the decision must address that gap.", "Assume all students have home internet.", "Treat connectivity as the vendor's responsibility."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Accessibility and equity are distinct. A tool can clear accessibility standards yet still impose unequal burdens through its access requirements. A defensible decision names that burden and how it will be addressed.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_5_3_accessible_means_equitable", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision", "cat531_supplement_usability_accessibility_and_equity"]}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_5_3_usability_equals_accessibility", "pattern": "usability_conflated_with_accessibility", "description": "Treating ease of use for typical students as proof that the tool is accessible to all learners", "trigger": "learner cites smooth general use as evidence of accessibility", "feedback": "Usability is the typical-user experience. Accessibility asks whether learners using assistive technology or accommodations can do the same tasks. Check that separately.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_5_3_wcag_badge_is_proof", "pattern": "conformance_badge_as_proof", "description": "Accepting a WCAG or accessibility marketing badge as proof of conformance for the classroom workflow", "trigger": "learner trusts an accessibility label without a report or task check", "feedback": "A badge is a claim. Ask for a conformance report and check the actual tasks your students perform before trusting it.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_5_3_automated_audit_is_complete", "pattern": "automated_audit_treated_as_complete", "description": "Treating an automated or AI accessibility scan as a complete accessibility clearance", "trigger": "learner reports 'no issues found' from an automated tool as final", "feedback": "Automated scans catch only some barriers. Add a task walkthrough with assistive technology and, where possible, disabled-user testing.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_5_3_accessible_means_equitable", "pattern": "accessible_assumed_equitable", "description": "Assuming that a tool clearing accessibility standards therefore poses no equity risk", "trigger": "learner stops the equity analysis once accessibility is confirmed", "feedback": "An accessible tool can still impose unequal burdens, such as home connectivity or device requirements. Name those burdens and how the decision addresses them.", "rail_action": "ask"}]}